WEBVTT

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:00.277
If you're a particle physicist,

00:03:00.277 --> 00:03:02.786
you want to know what happens 
when we smash things together.

00:03:02.786 --> 00:03:04.776
And dark matter is no exception.

00:03:04.776 --> 00:03:06.436
Well, how do we do this?

00:03:06.436 --> 00:03:07.927
To answer that question,

00:03:07.927 --> 00:03:09.320
I'm going to have to ask another one,

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.486
which is, what happens when galaxy clusters collide?

00:03:12.486 --> 00:03:14.796
Here is an image.

00:03:14.796 --> 00:03:17.987
Since galaxy clusters are representative

00:03:17.987 --> 00:03:21.111
slices of the universe, scaled-down versions.

00:03:21.111 --> 00:03:23.234
They are mostly made up of dark matter,

00:03:23.234 --> 00:03:25.787
and that's what you see in this bluish purple.

00:03:25.787 --> 00:03:27.404
The red represents the hot gas,

00:03:27.404 --> 00:03:29.371
and, of course, you can see many galaxies.

00:03:29.371 --> 00:03:31.736
What's happened is a particle accelerator